    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d937">

                <pb facs="0960" n="948" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg948"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/8701: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Yugoslavia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Belgrade</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-03-01T13:00:00+01:00">March 1, 1941—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:27 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>135. My 128, February 27, 4 p.m., paragraph 3.<anchor n="35" xml:id="d937-f1"/> I
                    left with the Prime Minister this morning a memorandum giving specific instances
                        <pb facs="0961" n="949" xml:id="pg949"/> of publication in official Avala
                    bulletin of D. N. B.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d937-f2"/> and Stefani<anchor n="36a"
                        xml:id="d937-f3"/> despatches from the United States containing distorted
                    and misleading accounts of conditions and especially with regard to the attitude
                    toward Lend-Lease Bill.</p>
                <p>The Prime Minister said that he would give instructions as far as Avala is
                    concerned. As to public opinion in Yugoslavia he said that propaganda of any
                    sort is useless as people well know our attitude toward Germany and that they
                    are wholeheartedly pro-British and American and anti-German. Nothing the Germans
                    can say will change this attitude. It is even necessary for the Yugoslav
                    Government at times to curb this attitude because of danger of creating trouble
                    with Germany.</p>
                <p>I pointed out that my complaint was justified especially by the Yugoslav
                    Government’s having made complaints regarding news despatches sent from
                    Yugoslavia by American correspondents despite fact that they were not published
                    in any United States Government organ.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lane</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
